<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Chera Perumals of Makotai</h1><p class="st-meta">800 CE – 1124 CE (approx.) · capital Mahodayapuram</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kingdom of the Chera Perumals of Kerala, ruling from Kodungallur, whose copper plates record grants to Christian and Jewish merchants.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the early 9th century a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chera-dynasty">Chera</a> kingdom re-emerged on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> coast, claiming the legacy of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/early-cheras">Early Cheras</a>. Its capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahodayapuram">Mahodayapuram</a>, also called Makotai, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kodungallur">Kodungallur</a>. Tradition identifies its early kings with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishnavism">Vaishnava</a> poet-saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulashekhara-alvar">Kulashekhara Alvar</a> and the Shaiva saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheraman-perumal-nayanar">Cheraman Perumal Nayanar</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Perumals ruled through local chiefs, the naduvazhis, and temple councils of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kodungallur">Kodungallur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kollam">Kollam</a> were major ports for the spice trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. The Tharisapalli plates of 849, issued under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sthanu-ravi">Sthanu Ravi</a>, granted privileges to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-thomas-christians">Syrian Christian</a> church in Kollam, and the Jewish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> plates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhaskara-ravi-varman">Bhaskara Ravi</a> gave rights to a Jewish merchant. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malayalam-language">Malayalam</a> began to emerge as a distinct language.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Long wars with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chola-empire">Chola Empire</a> in the 11th century weakened the kingdom. After the last ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-kulashekhara">Rama Kulashekhara</a>, around 1124, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> divided into principalities such as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-venad">Venad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calicut">Kozhikode</a>, later ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamorin">Zamorin</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/chera-perumals-of-makotai.jpg" alt="Chera Perumals of Makotai" width="320" height="393"><figcaption>Image: Sundar Lakshman · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:South_India_in_early_11th_century_AD.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 CE – 1124 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mahodayapuram</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sthanu Ravi, Bhaskara Ravi, Rama Kulashekhara</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Malayalam, Tamil</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kulasekhara dynasty, Later Cheras, Cheras of Mahodayapuram</dd></dl></aside></div>
